#{[17592186148486 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148486 #edb/ref 1061 632750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148486 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0e51654-69d7-5097-b27f-71f3c28bce97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148486 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148487 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148487 #edb/ref 1061 632753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148487 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99330381-c770-5a62-a3c7-a79ba13fcd0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148487 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148488 #edb/ref 1061 632754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148488 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5102e680-a474-5349-a6c9-27bc67303b7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148488 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148670 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose Ethan and Ryan are baseball players. In the previous season, $24\\%$ of Ethan’s hits were home runs, while the decimal proportion of Ryan’s hits that were home runs was $0.3$.\n\nLet’s determine whether either Ethan or Ryan hit home runs on more than half of their total hits.\n\n- *For Ethan*, we compare his statistic to one-half written as a percentage, $50\\%$. Since\n$24\\% < 50\\%$,\nless than half of Ethan’s hits were home runs.\n- *For Ryan*, we compare his statistic to one-half written as a decimal, $0.5$. Since\n$0.3 < 0.5$,\nless than half of Ryan’s hits were home runs.\n\nTherefore, neither player hit home runs on more than half of their hits.\n\nNow, let’s see more concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148670 #edb/ref 1065 14935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148670 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fbb4d977-7db5-579e-baf0-bb8e13b6c15a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148670 #edb/ref 1067 "Comparing Decimals and Percents in Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148671 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose Carlos and Henry have the same number of trading cards. A total of $32 \\%$ of Carlos's cards are rare cards, while\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nof Henry's cards are rare cards.\n\nWho has more rare cards?\n\nTo answer this, we need to *compare the fraction and the percentage*:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 32 \\%\n$$\n\nA common method is to convert the fraction to a percentage.\n\nFirst, we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nas an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 25}{4 \\times 25} = \\dfrac{25}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{100} = 25 \\%\n$$\n\nSince $25 \\% < 32 \\%$, we can conclude that Carlos has *more* rare cards than Carlos." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148671 #edb/ref 1065 14937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148671 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4be68676-8fcc-56ce-a8ef-96b2a954a03c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148671 #edb/ref 1067 "Comparing Fractions and Percents in Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148698 #edb/ref 1064 "When working with fractions, percents, and decimals, we often need to decide which number is greater or smaller. Since these numbers are written in different forms, it can be difficult to compare them directly.\n\nTo make comparisons easier, we can *convert them to a common form*.\n\nFor example, let's compare the percent $48\\%$ and the decimal $0.52$.\n\nOne way to do that is to convert the percent to a decimal. So, we divide the percent value by $100{:}$\n\n$$\n48 \\div 100 = 0.48\n$$\n\nThus, $48\\%$ is equivalent to $0.48$.\n\nNow, we can compare the two decimals:\n$0.48 < 0.52$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $48\\%$ is smaller than $0.52$.\n\nLet's see more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148698 #edb/ref 1065 2078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148698 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e6681afa-a432-5074-a46c-204561f2cf65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148698 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152124 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152124 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152124 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152124 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152124 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152124 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying Numbers as Natural" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152124 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152124 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f0075774-6bd8-54c9-b698-4aacd499fce5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152125 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152125 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152125 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152125 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152125 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying Numbers as Integers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152125 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152125 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ebf3f930-2768-5399-8470-48ac430065a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152126 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152126 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152126 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152126 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152126 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152126 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152126 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying Numbers as Rational" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152126 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152126 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f7063140-eebe-598c-b530-3c13509d359b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152127 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152127 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152127 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152127 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Rational Decimals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152127 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152127 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "be826645-6351-54ab-b57d-2c2a83a1b68d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1045 "Natural Numbers, Integers, and Rational Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186153002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152314 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "26b78e45-1997-563c-b456-d53ae655f749" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153002 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153002 #edb/ref 1061 172680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153002 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0fa6983b-4c2c-5789-8e95-585a4e680b9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153002 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153003 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153003 #edb/ref 1061 172681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153003 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc685578-51b3-5ffb-b8b8-a5cca744297f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153003 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153004 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153004 #edb/ref 1061 172684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153004 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bfd8720b-23e8-55fe-8c02-ac787976007a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153004 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153005 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153005 #edb/ref 1061 172685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153005 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34d04639-2557-5679-9ab1-fa22b4733ce3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153005 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153006 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153006 #edb/ref 1061 172688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153006 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc715ab1-8b8b-555b-bd73-97d8d33107c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153006 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153007 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153007 #edb/ref 1061 172689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153007 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba79c281-b939-528f-8605-0a6cd2aa6abd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153007 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153008 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153008 #edb/ref 1061 172692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153008 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8cd64bc9-e39e-5f5c-9c0d-618b3294cff3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153008 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153009 #edb/ref 1061 172693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153009 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c946365-2b4a-5987-84b7-84cce8c8a6f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153009 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153465 #edb/ref 1064 "The **natural numbers** are the positive whole numbers:\n${\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2},\\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots$\nWe also refer to them as the **counting numbers**.\n\nThere's some debate among mathematicians about whether zero should be considered a natural number. However, for our purposes, zero is *not* a natural number." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153465 #edb/ref 1065 9069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153465 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ddcec145-4774-553b-836d-76a4a5cd221e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153465 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153466 #edb/ref 1064 "When we extend the natural numbers by joining them with zero and the whole negative numbers, we get the **integers**.\n$\\overbrace{\\ldots, {\\color{red}-5},\\: {\\color{red}-4},\\: {\\color{red}-3},\\: {\\color{red}-2},\\: {\\color{red}-1},\\: {\\color{red}0}, \\: \\!\\underbrace{{\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2}, \\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots}_{\\large\\textrm{Natural numbers}}}^{\\large\\textrm{Integers}}$\n\nWe can represent the relationship between the natural numbers and integers using an inclusion diagram:\n\n![](images/00/00a2725bbe34eb8bc88f7f6dca6f22e624854a0cb469645eb2f4f9b79a38f846.png)\n\nNotice that the natural numbers are *contained within* the integers. In other words, *every* natural number is also an integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153466 #edb/ref 1065 9070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153466 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4109da7f-f9b5-5c94-8215-06f5ea408038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153466 #edb/ref 1067 "The Integers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153467 #edb/ref 1064 "The **rational numbers** consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers. For example:\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{2}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nEvery integer is also a rational number because every integer can be expressed as a ratio of two integers. For example,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{2}{1}}, \\qquad {\\color{red}-9} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{(-9)}{\\phantom{-}1}}\n$$\n\nLikewise, any mixed number is a rational number because it can be written as an improper fraction. For example,\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nis a rational number because\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2 \\times 3 + 1}{3} = \\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the rational numbers to our inclusion diagram.\n\n![](images/12/124ced6b317bdd2df4f967e6ea45e613b89d9fa136acb5edf8f7ed86fa8a4c06.png)\n\nNotice that the integers (and therefore the natural numbers) are contained within the rational numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153467 #edb/ref 1065 9071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153467 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3563ca37-8d82-5742-92b2-bb3918a71477" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153467 #edb/ref 1067 "The Rational Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153468 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the following rational number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nWe can find the decimal representation of this rational number by dividing $1$ by $3$ using long division, as follows:\n\n| $\\,\\,0 \\,. 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3$$\\:\\ldots$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$\\,\\ldots$ |\n| --- |\n\nNote that, in this case\n\n- the long division process never ends, and\n- the digit $3$ after the decimal point repeats *forever*.\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nThis is an example of a **repeating decimal**. A repeating decimal is a decimal where the digits after the decimal point continue forever but in a repeating pattern.\n\nIt's convenient to use the following special notation to indicate decimals that repeat indefinitely.\n\n$$\n0.\\overline{3} = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nSome decimals have two or more digits that repeat indefinitely. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.78^(―) &= 0.78787878… \\\\\n0.675^(―) &= 0.675675675…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome repeating decimals have non-repeating digits before the repeating part. For example:\n\n$$\n0.333\\overline{67} = 0.333\\,67\\,67\\,67\\,67\\ldots\n$$\n\nThe key takeaway for this section is that every repeating decimal is rational! In other words, every repeating decimal can be expressed as a ratio of two integers.\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.3^(―) &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n0.78^(―) &= \\frac{26}{33} \\\\\n0.33367^(―) &= \\frac{16517}{49500}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn a future lesson, we'll learn how to express a repeating decimal as a fraction. For now, you can verify these results using a calculator." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153468 #edb/ref 1065 9072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153468 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "28ff4afd-e8c7-511c-86a4-14d547e6c6d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153468 #edb/ref 1067 "Repeating Decimals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186168730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186168731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186168732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186168733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186168734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186168734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168729 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c4005020-e950-5306-a22c-5bc62b40c40b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168730 #edb/ref